    <string name="field_effort_name">Effort budget</string>
    <string name="field_effort_description">What the rest of the route still costs, in kilojoules rather than kilometres, split by item: the rolling terrain, then each remaining climb. The total comes from the estimated time of each item and the power you actually hold in that regime — two measured quantities, none assumed. Needs a power meter.</string>
    <string name="field_effort_label">STILL TO PAY</string>
    <string name="field_effort_waiting">Learning your pace</string>
    <string name="field_effort_climb">Climb %1$d — %2$s</string>
    <string name="field_effort_kilojoules">%1$d kJ</string>
    <string name="unit_kilojoule">kJ</string>
    <string name="field_bends_name">Bends</string>
    <string name="field_bends_description">The bends of the next three kilometres, on the road laid straight: each one becomes a bar reaching up or down for its direction, long and red for its radius. It is not a map — the minimap already does that — but the answer to the only question a descent raises: what is coming, and how tight.</string>
    <string name="field_bends_label">BENDS</string>
    <string name="field_bends_summary">%1$d over %2$s</string>
    <string name="field_bends_none">Nothing tight ahead</string>
    <string name="field_bends_left">LEFT</string>
    <string name="field_bends_right">RIGHT</string>
    <string name="field_bends_hairpin">HAIRPIN</string>
    <string name="field_bends_tight">TIGHT</string>
    <string name="field_bends_bend">BEND</string>
    <string name="field_context_name">Follows the ride</string>
    <string name="field_context_description">A field whose lower half changes with what the ride is doing: climbing, what is left to the summit and the shape of it; descending, the bends; near a resupply point, its distance; the rest of the time, speed and what remains. The upper half never moves, and a rail of dots says which state you are in.</string>
    <string name="field_context_to_summit">TO SUMMIT</string>
    <string name="field_context_since_last">SINCE LAST</string>
    <string name="field_context_remaining">REMAINING</string>
    <string name="field_surface_name">Surface</string>
    <string name="field_surface_description">What the route has underfoot for the next five kilometres, by laying the track over the on-board map: road, track, cycleway, each to the scale of its length. For gravel this is the information missing everywhere — not where you are, but when you leave the tarmac and for how long. Needs the offline map.</string>
    <string name="field_surface_label">SURFACE</string>
    <string name="field_surface_unknown">No way recognised</string>
    <string name="field_surface_road">Road</string>
    <string name="field_surface_trail">Track</string>
    <string name="field_surface_cycleway">Cycleway</string>
    <string name="field_surface_then">then %1$s</string>
    <string name="field_surface_next_trail">TRACK IN</string>
    <string name="field_surface_next_cycleway">CYCLEWAY IN</string>
    <string name="field_surface_next_road">ROAD IN</string>
    <string name="field_resupply_name">Reserve</string>
    <string name="field_resupply_description">Which resupply point is the last one before there is nothing. Not “where is the next water” — the other field says that — but the announcement turned around: the line carries the whole route, passed points in grey, the last useful one ringed in yellow, and to its right a red segment carrying nothing. That emptiness is what you look at. Needs a full page.</string>
